00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:40.010
We here at the Journey to the Microcosmos
headquarters have been very excited about

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:43.221
this ciliate, which is called Legendrea loyezae.

00:00:43.221 --> 00:00:48.940
James, our master of microscopes, found it
in a lake, which made him just one of five

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.210
people in human history who 
had ever found this organism.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:58.710
In our last episode, we talked about how his
discoveries managed to reconcile the contradictions

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.997
that had gone unexplained in the observations
previous scientists had made.

00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:09.259
What we didn’t talk about was the fact that
James and his collaborators, Dr. Demetra Andreou

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:15.060
and Professor Genoveva F. Esteban, were the
first people to gather genetic data about

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:21.030
the Legendrea genus, thanks in part to their
little Legendrea loyezae friends.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.770
Now when we say “genetic data,” we could
be referring to a lot of different things,

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:30.390
in part because genes are such an extensive,
varied landscape that humans are only in the

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.433
early days of being able to describe.

00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:38.300
Genes contain the instructions that tell our
cells how to make the proteins that carry

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:45.732
out our lives as they drive chemical reactions
or produce key structures or pass along information.

00:01:45.732 --> 00:01:52.160
And those instructions are written in the language
of DNA, a double helix whose components spell

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.540
out individual bits of a message, like letters
building up to a word that builds up to a

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.380
manual for life.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:06.049
To make that language comprehensible to us,
scientists use the letters A, T, G, and C

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:10.631
to represent the chemical bases that link
together to form that code.

00:02:10.631 --> 00:02:15.310
But within every living organism, whether
it is us or the organisms we’re watching,

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:22.630
that code is a living, churning, evolving
force that enshrines our identity in relation

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.647
to so many others.

00:02:24.647 --> 00:02:29.810
In our journey through the microcosmos, we have
often seen the way genetic material and the

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:36.580
use of phylogenetic techniques reveal new
ways to see an organism’s identity or to

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:43.409
upend a set of relationships we thought existed
in the microcosmos, like when scientists realized

00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:48.000
that the organisms they had gathered together
in the taxonomic category of “amoeba”

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.030
were not really so closely related after all.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:56.750
The idea is simple: let’s compare organisms
and see if we can figure out—based on their

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:00.800
similarities and differences—how closely
related they are.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.000
We can—and have—done this with microscopes
and extensive descriptions of what different

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.670
microbes look and act like.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:14.830
But like we said, genetics has often upended
what we have gleamed just through observation.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:19.890
But to study organisms at the genetic level,
we need their DNA.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:24.500
Which means that we need to be able to wade
through all the bits and pieces lying within

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:30.030
their tiny bodies to pick out something even
tinier—something we can’t just dig out

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.336
with a shovel.

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:32.736
So how?

00:03:32.736 --> 00:03:39.538
How does James manage to get the precious
DNA from Legendrea loyezae and other ciliates

00:03:39.538 --> 00:03:41.790
he is interested in studying?

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:46.700
He starts by grabbing the whole organism,
separating it from the crowd in a manner similar

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.360
to what you can see him doing here with some
other microbes.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:55.200
There they are, just chilling, until James
appears with a tool called a micropipette

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.421
that draws them right up.

00:03:57.421 --> 00:04:01.560
After taking the ciliate he is interested
in, it’s time for James to give it a little

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.760
bath to make sure that it is clean and
removed any organism that might cloud

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.250
up the results.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:13.480
He starts by squirting the organism into a
clean drop of water and letting it swim around

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.620
for a while.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.759
Then, grabbing a new, clean micropipette,

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:20.269
James repeats the process again.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:22.697
And again, and again, and again.

00:04:22.697 --> 00:04:30.180
Each time, using a new micropipette to drop
the organism into a fresh drop of water.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.960
The goal is to get the ciliate very, very
clean.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.830
And that means James sometimes has to leave
them swimming for as long as 30 minutes in

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:40.990
that drop of water.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.550
And that’s not just to scrub their outsides.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:48.340
The insides need to be empty of any ciliates
they might have eaten, that are now taking

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.076
up residence inside of a food vacuole.

00:04:51.076 --> 00:04:57.300
Yes, even a master of microscopes has to wait
around a bit for a ciliate to poop sometimes.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.810
When he’s done and the ciliates are cleaned
up, James freezes them in a tiny tube, getting

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.610
them ready for the next step: isolating the
DNA.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:11.450
The process is simple: he takes the tube out
of the freezer and lets it thaw out.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.551
And then he puts it back into the freezer
and lets it ice up again.

00:05:15.551 --> 00:05:18.770
Just like the washing, this is a repetitive
process.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:24.896
Over and over again, the organism is frozen,
then thawed, frozen, then thawed.

00:05:25.695 --> 00:05:29.710
Each time, the cell changes a little bit.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:35.683
Ice pierces through the membrane, and the
insides of the cell begin to disintegrate.

00:05:35.683 --> 00:05:41.205
Buried in the cellular soup that remains
is the organism’s DNA.

00:05:41.205 --> 00:05:45.669
Now separating that DNA requires a technique that
you have probably heard a lot about in the

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:50.930
past few years: PCR, which stands for polymerase
chain reaction.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:58.298
The goal of PCR is to take a small amount of DNA 
and then make many, many more copies of it.

00:05:58.298 --> 00:06:04.490
During the pandemic, we relied on PCR to find
fragments of viral RNA in our mucus, and then

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:09.810
to amplify it so that those small quantities
of genetic material could be detectable.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:14.840
But PCR is so important that even describing
it as a tool to save lives during a pandemic

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.160
feels like we are underselling it.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.460
It is one of the tools whose premise is
so simple that it can be used and adapted

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.120
for any number of applications.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:32.199
Since its discovery in 1985, PCR has been
fundamental to the work of scientists who

00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:37.810
are doing everything from hunting down proteins
to engineering new technologies to creating

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.780
new medical therapies.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:44.840
For today though, PCR’s main use to us is
to gather small segments of the Legendrea

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:51.120
loyezae’s DNA that can be used to find where
it belongs taxonomically.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:57.300
And as with the previous steps that James
had to undertake, there is a lot of repetition.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:02.620
The ciliate that was cleaned over and over
again, and then frozen and thawed over and

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:09.220
over again will now have its contents heated
and cooled in a mixture that will tear apart

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:16.580
the strands of DNA and find the parts that
James wants to amplify, over and over again.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:22.169
With each repetition, the number of DNA fragments
goes up exponentially so that in the end,

00:07:22.169 --> 00:07:28.500
James is holding a tube full of them, ready
to be sent off so that a machine can determine

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:35.074
its sequence of As, Ts, Cs, and Gs for scientists
to study further.

00:07:35.074 --> 00:07:41.500
With his prized DNA, James was able to find
a family for the Legendrea loyezae: the family

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:44.480
Spathidiidae, Order Haptorida.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.830
He was even able to find a sibling: Epispathidium
papilliferum.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:55.889
They even have similar protruding structures,
making their family resemblance apparent beyond

00:07:55.889 --> 00:07:58.139
the genetic similarities.

00:07:58.139 --> 00:08:03.770
This is a relationship that the early observers
of Legendrea loyezae might not have been able

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:04.770
to infer.

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:10.050
And even if they did, the tools to verify
them didn’t exist.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:14.759
Each time we visit the microcosmos, it’s
with knowledge and tools

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:16.775
that we did not have before.

00:08:16.775 --> 00:08:22.490
That is why it matters to discover again
what someone found once before, to dig through

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.322
old texts and familiar ponds.

00:08:25.322 --> 00:08:31.623
Because buried deep within, there is always
something unknown remaining to be found.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:42.133
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

